A||||9||86GT-FstBk||lights, chimmin, and no sound||||5||
Z||000000||86GT-FstBk||09-02-2000||10:07 PM||||OK here it is.  My courtesy lights stay on, I blow my fuse all the time even when I put a new one in.  My chime starts up when I turn the dimmer down and when I turn the dimmer up it starts to chime.  I installed my new CD player today and it powers up but no sound.  I'm really stumped about the sound.  Help anybody help.  Thanks Brian.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-03-2000||12:53 AM||||First things first - the fuse blows because you have a short circuit somewhere. In other words, the fuses are just doing their job. Locate the short, and fix it.<P>If your problem is the usual "my courtesy lights come on when I shut the door" thing, then fuse that keeps blowing is the one that feeds most of the "always hot" wires -  a bunch of spliced-together <B>orange</B> wires that power the horn, radio "keep-alive," cigarette lighter, and a few other things.  Think the fuse is labelled "BAT" in the fuse panel (probably so named, because it was designed to drive people batty  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Temporary fix for the "lights stay on" problem (so you don't run down your battery):  Pull the "CTSY/LID" fuse.  The lights should go out. <P>If you just installed the CD player when the problem showed up, it might be a good idea to look in that area first, for the short.<P>Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   ||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||09-03-2000||11:57 AM||||If you mean the chime dies when dimmer off and chime starts as dimmer is turned up......<P>The chime unit thinks the park/head lights are on and the car is off. This function is fed from the dimmer & dash lights. Why I don't know. If the dimmer is all the way down it cuts off the chime as well. That is how it works on all Fiero. (Most people don't notice that because they don't turn the dimmer all the way down.)<P>Doesn't Fiero dimmer also have a switch to activate courtesy lights? I can't remember right now. You could have a bad dimmer knob. That would account for both light and chime.<P>This of course assumes the problem started before the new radio went in. You did use an adaptor to hook it up. Tell us you didn't cut of the original plugs. The adaptor to the factory plug is only about $7 at K mart. A little more else where.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||09-03-2000||12:33 PM||||Ogre: Yes - turn the dimmer switch all the way up, past a little detent, and the interior lights (dome+ courtesy) come on.<P>Didn't know that the dingy box "read" the dash lights circuit to determine that the lights are on.  Thanks - someday I'll be glad you told me this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||01:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>Ogre: Yes - turn the dimmer switch all the way up, past a little detent, and the interior lights (dome+ courtesy) come on.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't have the slightest idea how to help you but this is supposed to happen.  I don't think this is what ogre meant.  I think he meant, if your dimmer switch is all the way off, you get no chime and it starts to chime once you begin to turn the dimmer up...something like that.<P>That concludes my part of the training session. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||01:30 PM||||Stu--Just realized you weren't the one with the problem.  (Duh! to me.)  Oh, well.  What I said still applies. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||86GT-FstBk||09-03-2000||06:12 PM||||Ogre, what adapter plug are you talking about?  I bought my fiero without a radio in it and it just had wires and no harness.  Oh about the chimmin and lights, that is fixed but my radio problem still exists.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||09-03-2000||11:04 PM||||You say the CD player powers up, but won't make any sound.  Did you put a CD in it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ? <P>OE wires in the harness to the speakers in the car are as follows (I <B>think</B>:<P>Rear speakers<BR>LR speaker: Brown wire+Yellow wire.<BR>RR speaker: 2 Blues.<P>Front speakers<BR>LF speaker: Grey wire+Tan wire.<BR>RF speaker: Dark green wire+Light green wire<P>If you find a bundle of wires with those colors, they are probably the ones you should use. <P>Maybe you could introduce a "guest speaker."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> In other words, hook an extra speaker to one of the CD player's speaker outputs.  That's what I'd try next.  If the CD player talks through the "guest" speaker, you know the player works, and you just need to get the speaker outputs connected to the speakers.<P>Wait a minute- you said the previous owner cut off the radio connectors?  Better check to see if they didn't take the speakers out, too!  ||||reg||12
Z||000008||86GT-FstBk||09-04-2000||11:14 AM||||OK Stu,  now I'm not a complete idiot.  Yes I put a CD in it and tried the radio too.  I know the speakers are there because I had the whole interior out.  I also put new front speakers in.  But hey thanks for the comedy.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-04-2000||02:52 PM||||The best thing to do in a case like this is go to a scrap yard and get the OE radio plug from another car and use it to repair the damage. Likely if it comes from another Fiero you can simply match the colors. (The colors should hold true for all Fiero but may be diferant in other GM cars.)<P>Then you go to K mart etc and get an adaptor to connect the new radio into the factory plug. The adaptor will have all the notes you need to hook up any standard radio. Later if you pull out your new radio to sell the car all you'll need is to plug in an OE GM radio.||||reg||1
